{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789059562,"nonce":"CBEAsEjxw8VjNNrPY6zN+iPwFJQ=","message":"Reproducer attempt: does NOT reproduce. Recorded so it is not rebuilt.\n\nFiberRootLost.hx (kept on the NUC at ~/repro) takes the failing test's ingredients: ten Thread.createWithEventLoop workers each running a Timer plus a Timer.delay, allocation churn to place a collection in the window, an Array\u003cVoid-\u003eVoid\u003e held across rounds, and the array iterated from INSIDE a main-loop Timer.delay -- the same EventLoop -\u003e Timer -\u003e callbacks path the real trace shows. Built with ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1 and sqlite.wasm beside the output.\n\n0 failures in 120 runs. At the threads suite's rate (about 1 in 60) that is P ~= 0.13, so it is not conclusive, but it is more likely that an ingredient is missing than that it was luck.\n\nTwo of my earlier runs of this reproducer were worthless for a different reason and should be ignored if anyone reads the history: 240 runs executed WITHOUT the side module loaded at all, because `ash --build f.wasm` -- an output name with no directory component -- silently discovered no side modules. That is a real bug, fixed in 74d66db; before it, the program linked the narrow ABI and refused every library at run time. Any wasm reproduction built that way is testing the passing configuration.\n\nSo the threads suite remains the only known reproducer: bin/threads.wasm-fibers.wasm under ash-wasm-run with bin/sqlite.wasm beside it, about 1 run in 60 to 120.\n\nWhat is still missing from the small program, most likely: utest's Async holds its callbacks array on an object that outlives several event-loop turns and is reached through a Dispatcher, and the failing element is reached after a done() that also stops a Timer. The reproducer holds the array in a static and never stops a timer from inside the same callback that reads it.","files":null}]}